				136,
				1
			],
			"Message": "fix(Land): Advance Element submodule — webview first-paint race, unhandled IPC notifications\n\nAdvance the Element submodule pointer to 285ddc3a, integrating fixes for a webview panel first-paint race condition and four IPC notification handlers missing from the Mountain dispatch table.\n\nCocoon now sends `viewId: ViewType` alongside `handle` in `webview.setHtml` and `webview.postMessage` payloads. The `webview.create` acknowledgement could arrive before `IOverlayWebview` bound the handle to a WebviewInput, causing the initial `setHtml` to park in `ParkedHtml` and `postMessage` to miss its target. The viewType fallback key in Sky's `InstallWebview.ts` resolves the race.\n\nMountain forwards four previously unhandled IPC notifications: `ScanPathConfigure` to scan `~/.land/extensions` for pre-migration extension discovery; `debug.dap-response` from Cocoon inline-implementation adapters to the workbench via `sky://debug/dap-message`, enabling RawDebugSession DAP response correlation by `request_seq`; `setStatusBarText` mutations from extension `StatusBarItem.text()` setters, which previously froze the status bar text after initial create; and `terminate_task`/`Task.Terminate` to the extension host task service, enabling `vscode.tasks.executeTask().terminate()` to stop running tasks with a graceful fallback.\n\nWebview panels now render correctly on first paint, status bar text updates dynamically, DAP debug sessions receive response correlation, extension task cancellation works end to end, and pre-migration extension directories are discovered on clean installs.",
			"SHA": "5400f2c7573668d80c766593a270221f3aacd6bd"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-25T21:29:31Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				136,
				1
			],
			"Message": "fix(Land): Advance Element submodule — breaker skips failure count when Open\n\nAdvance the Element submodule pointer to e799e065, integrating a Cocoon circuit breaker fix that prevents in-flight gRPC completions from accumulating failure counts after the breaker has already tripped Open.\n\nCocoon's CheckCircuitBreaker was incrementing the failure counter unconditionally on every error completion, even when the breaker state was already Open. In-flight requests arriving after the trip could push the counter well beyond the intended threshold (observed at N≥19) and produce noisy \"transition from=OPEN to=Open failures=N\" debug-log lines on every additional error.\n\nThe fix returns early when the state is Open, logging the post-trip completion at debug level without counting it. Recovery remains driven by the existing timeout-based transition to HalfOpen, unchanged.\n\nThis eliminates dev-log chatter and keeps the failure counter accurate within the Closed and HalfOpen observation windows that actually drive breaker state transitions.",
			"SHA": "3a783a02027449085b4eb76da4d7c269c7862b31"
		}
	],
